explanation:
Answer (B) understates the level of tension depicted in both cartoons, while (D) overstates it. A rivalry is definitely unfolding, making (B) incorrect, but warfare is not directly alluded to (and, again, the USSR did not yet have atomic weapons), so (D) is also wrong. Both cartoons are intended for English-speaking audiences (the first appeared in American newspapers, the second in the British magazine 
Punch
), and while there were some Western critics of Washington’s and London’s Cold War diplomacy, the fact that the cartoons are intended for a wide Western audience—not to mention their depiction of Stalin as brash and predatory (especially in the first cartoon)—makes (A) a very unlikely pick. Both cartoons reflect the Anglo-American consensus at the time that Cold War tensions were the direct result of Soviet aggression, as in (C).
